unit Bobs;

// ============================================================================
// Bobs -- Blitter OBject primitives, extracted from main.ras.
//
// CROSS-FILE IDENTIFIER RULES (learned empirically while doing this
// extraction -- confirmed via the real headless CLI compiler, not just the
// IDE's own diagnostics, which gave a false "'Interface' expected" error on
// this file's very first line that the real compiler never raised):
//
//   1. A unit has its OWN separate variable namespace. Code inside this unit
//      cannot read/write a var declared in main.ras by its plain name --
//      confirmed by trying it (moving ClampWordCol here without also moving
//      blk_result/shift_result failed to parse with "Could not find
//      variable 'Bobs_blk_result'"). Any state this unit's procedures touch
//      has to be declared HERE, in this unit's own var block.
//   2. From main.ras, call a procedure declared here as "Bobs::ProcName(...)"
//      (matches the existing Graphics::SetupDefaultScreen convention).
//   3. From main.ras, READ OR WRITE a variable declared here as
//      "Bobs_varname" (underscore, NOT "Bobs::varname" -- confirmed by the
//      compiler's own suggested fix: "Could not find variable 'blk_result'.
//      Did you mean 'Bobs_blk_result'?"). This is the SAME mangled name the
//      compiler generates internally for every identifier this unit owns.
//   4. Raw asm("...") text is NOT rewritten by the compiler -- it only
//      resolves plain Pascal-level references. Any identifier OWNED BY THIS
//      UNIT that appears inside an asm() string (including this unit's own
//      locals, not just cross-file ones) must be written using its real
//      mangled name (e.g. "Bobs_draw_target", not "draw_target") or the
//      assembler fails with "undefined symbol". This matches how keys.tru
//      itself writes "Keys_key" (not "key") inside its own asm block.
//
// NUM_PLANES/DISPLAY_BITPLANES/ROW_STRIDE below are DUPLICATED from
// main.ras, not moved -- they're fundamental screen-layout facts the
// background-drawing code (FastFillBand/BlitFillRect) also depends on, so
// moving them would mean renaming ~200 unrelated call sites across the rest
// of the file for no benefit to the bob mechanism itself. Since they are
// true compile-time consts (never reassigned), two independently-declared
// copies with the same value carry no runtime risk -- only a manual-sync
// risk if one is ever changed without the other, same class of risk this
// project already accepts for gamecode.ras. THE THREE CONST VALUES BELOW
// MUST BE KEPT IDENTICAL TO main.ras's OWN COPIES.
//
// draw_target and bg_pristine, in contrast, ARE genuinely moved (single
// source of truth here) rather than duplicated -- they're mutable state
// written by main.ras (FlipBuffer sets draw_target; CaptureBgPristine fills
// bg_pristine) and read by this unit's own blit/restore procedures, so a
// duplicate copy would silently diverge. main.ras now reads/writes them as
// Bobs_draw_target / Bobs_bg_pristine (rule 3 above).
// ============================================================================

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TRSE / 68000 COMPILER QUIRKS THIS UNIT WORKS AROUND -- self-contained
// reference for the "Quirk 1-7" mentions further down. Most were found by
// direct inspection of the generated 68000 assembly, not guessed from
// symptoms alone; most are silent (no warning, no assemble-time error)
// and only produce a visibly wrong result under specific conditions, so
// casual testing can miss them (Quirk 7 is the exception -- it fails
// loudly, at compile time).
//
// QUIRK 1 -- a pointer expression is only safe with EXACTLY one inline "*"
// -- not zero, not more than one. Two distinct, confirmed failure modes on
// either side of that one safe count:
// (a) ZERO inline multiplications -- a plain "#symbol + variable" addition,
//     the simplest-LOOKING pointer expression there is -- compiles to a
//     truncating 16-bit "add.w" after loading the full 32-bit symbol
//     address, silently dropping any carry into the upper word whenever
//     the addition overflows the low word. Confirmed via direct assembly
//     inspection for two unrelated symbol/offset pairs in this codebase's
//     history (worth treating as a general rule, not a one-off).
// (b) MORE THAN ONE inline multiplication chained by addition compiles to
//     a running sum that gets "checkpointed" through a 16-bit "move.w"
//     between multiplication steps -- which, unlike the add.w case above,
//     doesn't just drop a carry, it discards the ALREADY-ACCUMULATED upper
//     word outright, even on terms that had nothing to do with the second
//     multiplication.
// Only an expression with EXACTLY one inline multiplication reliably
// compiles to genuine 32-bit code (mulu.w feeding an add.l). This is
// deliberately counterintuitive to flag: the plain, multiplication-free
// addition in (a) looks like the SAFEST possible shape and is actually
// broken, while adding a multiplication nothing in the logic actually
// needs can be what makes an expression correct.
// RULE: don't try to force "exactly one multiplication" by padding an
// expression with an arbitrary "*1" or similar -- instead precompute the
// ENTIRE offset into a single plain integer variable first, using ordinary
// integer arithmetic (unrestricted -- integer math isn't pointer
// arithmetic and doesn't hit this issue at all, regardless of how many
// "*" it contains), then hand-roll the final pointer combine in inline asm
// with an explicit "add.l": load the base via "move.l #symbol,d0" (or the
// variable holding it), zero-extend the precomputed offset into a second
// register via "moveq #0,d1 / move.w offsetvar,d1", "add.l d1,d0", then
// store d0. This sidesteps TRSE's pointer-expression codegen for the
// risky step entirely and is unconditionally safe regardless of term
// count or multiplication count on either side of it.
//
// QUIRK 2 -- "X<0"/"X>=0" against literal 0 use the wrong (unsigned) branch.
// "if (X<0) then ..." compiles to a CMP against 0 followed by BHS (Branch
// if Higher-or-Same), which tests the CARRY flag -- an unsigned
// comparison. Comparing anything against literal 0 with CMP can never set
// carry (0 is the unsigned minimum), so BHS is unconditionally taken and
// the "X<0" branch never actually fires, for any input, ever. The mirror
// case, "if (X>=0)", compiles to CMP+BLO, which for the same reason is
// unconditionally taken too -- so that branch always runs regardless of
// X's real sign. Workaround used throughout this unit: bypass the
// comparison codegen entirely with a few lines of inline asm that test the
// sign bit directly via BPL/BMI (which read the N flag -- a true signed
// test).
//
// QUIRK 3 -- a pointer VARIABLE (not a "#symbol") as the base of a 3-term
// pointer expression ("base + mult + plainvar") makes TRSE emit a
// truncating 16-bit "add.w" for the final combine, where the IDENTICALLY
// SHAPED expression with a "#symbol" base correctly emits a full "add.l".
// Confirmed by diffing the generated assembly for the two cases side by
// side -- swapping a "#screen"-style base for a "pointer of byte" variable
// holding that same address was the only difference between a working and
// a silently-broken build. This is a genuinely separate trigger from
// Quirk 1 (the expression here has only one inline multiplication, which
// Quirk 1's own rule says should be safe), not a restatement of it.
// Workaround: precompute the offset ("mult + plainvar") into its own plain
// integer first, then hand-roll the final pointer add in inline asm with
// an explicit "add.l", bypassing the pointer-expression codegen for that
// one step.
//
// QUIRK 3 EXTENDED -- the ZERO-multiplication case ("base + plainvar", no
// "mult" term at all) is NOT reliably safe either, despite an earlier,
// now-corrected belief that it was. That belief came from ONE working
// example (Bobs::BuildBoxMaskInset's own "irp := ispr + ibyteoff", which
// does compile to a correct "add.l") being generalized into a rule
// without testing a second one. Writing three MORE procedures using the
// exact same source-level shape ("ptr := paramptr + offsetvar", parameter
// base, zero multiplications, one precomputed trailing plain variable)
// produced THREE DIFFERENT results from the SAME compiler on the SAME
// shape in the SAME file: one compiled safely (offset zero-extended into
// a register first, THEN the pointer variable added via "add.l" -- the
// same order BuildBoxMaskInset's own working example uses), and TWO
// compiled unsafely (the pointer variable loaded first, then the offset
// added via a truncating "add.w" -- Quirk 1's failure shape, just with a
// variable base instead of a "#symbol" one). All three were textually
// near-identical at the Pascal level; nothing about the source
// distinguishes the safe one from the unsafe two. CONCLUSION: this shape
// cannot be judged safe by matching it against any previously-observed
// working example, including ones in this very file -- Bobs::BuildBoxMask
// and Bobs::BuildBoxMaskInset's own internal pointer combines use exactly
// this shape and currently compile safely, but that should be read as
// "confirmed safe as of the last time each was actually rebuilt and
// checked," not as a guarantee that holds if either is ever edited.
// REVISED WORKAROUND, superseding the "precompute the offset, then a
// plain TRSE pointer-expression add" advice for the zero-multiplication
// case specifically: don't use a TRSE-level "ptr := paramptr + offset;"
// assignment at all when the base is a variable. Hand-roll the ENTIRE
// combine in inline asm instead -- zero-extend the offset into a
// register ("moveq #0,d0 / move.w offsetvar,d0"), then "add.l
// paramptr,d0", then store d0 into the destination pointer variable --
// bypassing TRSE's pointer-expression codegen completely rather than
// trusting it to pick the same codegen path twice.
//
// QUIRK 4 -- "if (CONST=literal)" against a named compile-time const can
// compile with the comparison elided entirely, unconditionally running the
// "true" branch regardless of the const's actual declared value. Confirmed
// by direct inspection of the generated assembly: the "true" block's code
// appeared as a plain, unconditional fall-through with no CMP/branch
// instruction anywhere before it, and the "else" block's label was
// confirmed (via a whole-file search of the generated assembly) to never
// be referenced by any branch instruction at all -- permanently dead code,
// unreachable regardless of the const's value. Only reproduced so far for
// a named "const" compared against a literal; a plain variable compared
// the same way is NOT affected (forces a genuine runtime comparison).
// Workaround: any value that will ever be the subject of an "if"
// comparison should be declared as a plain initialized variable, not a
// "const" -- e.g. "var MODE:integer = 0;" instead of
// "const MODE:integer = 0;". A value only ever used as a plain argument
// (never compared with "if") is unaffected and can stay a real const.
//
// QUIRK 5 -- assigning a literal whose high bit is set (i.e. one that
// reads as NEGATIVE in signed 16-bit, such as $8400 or $FFFE) to a
// variable, inside an if/then/else branch, can compile as if it were an
// unsigned byte constant -- without sign-extension. E.g. assigning -2 to
// an integer variable inside a branch compiled to "move.w #$fe,dest"
// (storing +254) instead of "move.w #$fffe,dest" (the correct -2).
// Workaround used in this unit: write such values as raw immediates
// directly in inline asm rather than through a plain TRSE assignment
// inside a conditional.
//
// QUIRK 6 -- "for X:=A to B do" is a TEST-AFTER (do-while) loop: it always
// executes the body at least once, with X=A, BEFORE ever checking the
// bound -- even when B<=A at that point (e.g. a runtime-computed bound of
// 0, meaning "nothing to iterate"). Confirmed by direct inspection of the
// generated assembly for a for-loop whose bound was an array read that
// could legitimately be 0: the compiled shape falls straight from
// initialization into the loop BODY (no test first), and only AFTER
// running the body does it increment the counter, compare against the
// bound, and branch back if not yet equal -- classic do-while structure,
// not the test-before shape "for X:=A to B do runs X=A..B-1" (this
// project's own longstanding shorthand for this loop's behavior, still
// correct for the ordinary B>A case, but incomplete: it doesn't cover
// what happens when B<=A). This went undiscovered for a long time because
// every single pre-existing for-loop bound in this codebase -- both
// compile-time consts (NUM_PLANES, NUM_ENEMIES, etc.) and runtime
// parameters (e.g. BuildBoxMask's own mhgt/mboxw) -- always happened to
// be a positive value on every actual call, so the body always needed to
// run at least once anyway and the missing test-before check never
// mattered. The failure mode when it DOES matter is severe and silent: an
// unconditional first iteration processing GARBAGE data (whatever
// uninitialized memory an index of 0 happens to read, if nothing was ever
// written there), and if the bound stays fixed at a value the loop
// counter can never again equal by simple +1 increments (e.g. bound
// stuck at 0 while the counter counts up from 0), the loop keeps running
// until the counter's own integer width wraps back around to match it --
// 65536 iterations for a 16-bit counter, each one whatever the loop body
// does (a full blitter round-trip in RestoreDirty's own case below,
// making one call take on the order of seconds and corrupt chip memory
// at a garbage-derived address on every one of those 65536 passes).
// Workaround (see RestoreDirty's own fix for a worked example): before
// any for-loop whose bound could legitimately be 0 at runtime, precompute
// the bound into a plain variable, then use raw inline asm ("tst.w
// varname" / "beq SkipLabel") to jump past the ENTIRE for-loop when that
// variable is zero, landing on a label placed immediately after the
// loop's closing "end;". Deliberately NOT a TRSE-level "if (bound>0)"
// guard -- Quirk 2 already established that comparisons against literal 0
// are a hazard area for this compiler, and TST+BEQ is unambiguous
// (tests the Z flag directly, no signed/unsigned interpretation involved)
// regardless of whether that risk turns out to extend to ">0" as well.
//
// QUIRK 7 -- a procedure CANNOT call another procedure declared LATER in
// the same unit/file -- there is no intra-unit forward reference support,
// despite this project's own main.ras documenting that "TRSE does support
// forward references" elsewhere. Confirmed by direct test: adding a call
// to BlitterNastyOn() (declared further down this file) from inside
// FlipBuffer (declared earlier) failed the headless build outright with
// "Could not find variable 'Bobs_BlitterNastyOn'" -- the same error shape
// this file's own header rules (see "cross-file identifier rules" near
// the top) describe for a genuinely undeclared identifier, not a
// deferred/forward one. This is scoped to a PLAIN call within the same
// unit -- it does NOT affect cross-unit calls (main.ras/tutorial.ras
// calling any Bobs::ProcName works regardless of where in this file that
// procedure sits, since @use imports the whole unit's declarations at
// once rather than sequentially) -- so the earlier "forward references
// work" note was evidently never exercised for THIS specific shape
// (same-file, procedure-to-later-procedure). Workaround: either declare
// the callee before the caller (reorder the file), or call it from
// outside the unit instead (e.g. from the caller's own Main(), the same
// way BlitterNastyOn is meant to be invoked once at startup anyway).
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

var
	const NUM_PLANES:integer = 3;
	const DISPLAY_BITPLANES:integer = 3;
	const ROW_STRIDE:integer = 40*DISPLAY_BITPLANES;

	bittable : array[8] of byte = (128,64,32,16,8,4,2,1);

	// Moved from main.ras -- single source of truth. main.ras's FlipBuffer
	// writes this (as Bobs_draw_target) to point at whichever of
	// #screen/#screen2 is currently the back buffer; every blit procedure
	// below reads it to know where to draw.
	draw_target : pointer of byte;

	// Moved from main.ras -- single source of truth. main.ras's
	// CaptureBgPristine fills this (as Bobs_bg_pristine) once at startup;
	// RestoreFromPristine/RestoreFromPristinePerPlane below read it every
	// frame.
	bg_pristine : array[ROW_STRIDE*256] of byte chipmem;

	// Results of ClampWordCol(): blk_result is the word-aligned byte
	// column; shift_result (0-15) is how many bits into that word the true
	// pixel position sits -- used by BlitStampMasked's per-pixel shifted
	// draw. Restore ignores shift_result (it always wants shift=0: put back
	// exactly what was captured).
	blk_result : integer;
	shift_result : integer;

	// Raster line (VP, 0-255) at which InstallNastyCopper's copper program
	// hands blitter bus-priority (BLTPRI) FROM the CPU TO the blitter for
	// the rest of the frame -- see the "Blitter Nasty" section and
	// InstallNastyCopper's own comment near the end of this file for the
	// full mechanism.
	nasty_on_line : integer = 0;

	// Which of FlipBuffer's two given buffers (0=first, 1=second argument)
	// is currently the BACK buffer -- owned here so FlipBuffer/FlipToBuffer
	// can be a fully self-contained double-buffering primitive, called as
	// Bobs::FlipBuffer(#screen, #screen2) with no caller-side bookkeeping
	// needed beyond that. NOTE: this is a SEPARATE variable from any
	// caller's own "cur_buf" (e.g. main.ras's own, in the strider project
	// this unit was extracted from, which still runs its OWN FlipBuffer
	// with its OWN cur_buf, since it also has to index player/attack
	// position history that has nothing to do with bobs) -- callers that
	// need to know which buffer is current for their OWN indexing (e.g.
	// tutorial.ras's per-buffer bob position history) read it as
	// Bobs_cur_buf, same as any other variable this unit owns.
	cur_buf : integer = 1;

	// ------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Automatic dirty-rect tracking state -- see the section of the same
	// name, right after RestoreFromPristinePerPlane below, for the full
	// mechanism, usage sketch, and known limitations. Declared here,
	// alongside this unit's other state, since TRSE vars all live in one
	// per-unit var block regardless of which procedure uses them.
	// ------------------------------------------------------------------
	const DIRTY_MAX:integer = 16;   // per-buffer capacity; bump if a caller draws more bobs per frame than this

	dirty_blk   : array[2*DIRTY_MAX] of integer;
	dirty_y     : array[2*DIRTY_MAX] of integer;
	dirty_ww    : array[2*DIRTY_MAX] of integer;
	dirty_wh    : array[2*DIRTY_MAX] of integer;
	dirty_np    : array[2*DIRTY_MAX] of integer;
	dirty_count : array[2] of integer = (0,0);

// Rounds a pixel-space left edge down to the nearest WORD boundary (even
// byte column), which the blitter's pointers require. This can land up
// to 15px left of wpixel_left (vs. up to 7px for plain byte-rounding) --
// callers must size their sprite/mask buffers wide enough to absorb that
// (see BuildBoxMask's own sizing note below for the exact rule).
procedure ClampWordCol(wpixel_left, wwidth_bytes : integer);
begin
	blk_result := (wpixel_left >> 4) << 1;
	shift_result := wpixel_left & 15;
	// A plain "if (blk_result<0) then blk_result:=0;" hits Quirk 2 above
	// (see the compiler-quirks reference near the top of this file) --
	// it would silently never fire, for any input. Bypassed with inline
	// asm testing the sign bit via BPL instead, which is unaffected by
	// that bug.
	// shift_result is reset to 0 in both clamp paths below (here, and in
	// the right-edge clamp further down) because once either clamp has
	// overridden blk_result to a forced/pinned column, the true sub-pixel
	// offset that shift_result would otherwise encode no longer describes
	// where the blit is actually landing -- leaving it un-reset would feed
	// BlitStampMasked a shift amount for a position the entity isn't
	// really at, visibly displaced from where the destination word column
	// says it should be (e.g. a "teleport" at the screen edge, once
	// diagnosed).
	asm("
		move.w Bobs_blk_result,d0
		bpl ClampWordColNoNeg
		moveq #0,d0
		move.w d0,Bobs_blk_result
		move.w d0,Bobs_shift_result
	ClampWordColNoNeg:
	");
	if (blk_result>40-wwidth_bytes) then begin
		blk_result:=40-wwidth_bytes;
		shift_result:=0;
	end;
end;

// Fills a sprite (color/B-channel) buffer with a solid color pattern.
// REWRITTEN for the interleaved-bitplane layout and the single-blit
// entity draw (see BlitStampMasked's own rewrite comment for the full
// mechanism this feeds): instead of NUM_PLANES separate contiguous
// whole-sprite blocks (one flat run of wbytes*hgt bytes per plane), this
// now writes hgt REAL rows, and for each row, DISPLAY_BITPLANES separate
// (wbytes+2)-byte chunks back to back (one per plane, real AND padding --
// see DISPLAY_BITPLANES's own comment) -- wbytes of real fill value, plus
// 1 extra WORD (2 bytes) of the SAME fill value as a genuine (not
// borrowed) barrel-shift lookahead slot. No hidden padding ROW anymore --
// hgt is now the TRUE row count, not row count+1 -- since every chunk
// already carries its own lookahead word, nothing needs to borrow from a
// "next" row/plane the way the old true-planar version did (borrowing
// across a PLANE boundary here would be actively wrong: two different
// planes can have different fill values -- see BlitStampMasked's
// comment).
// Colors fit a 3-bit budget (0-7) across NUM_PLANES=3 real planes;
// "(bscolor>>bp)&1" naturally evaluates to 0 for bp>=3, so the padding
// planes (bp from NUM_PLANES up to DISPLAY_BITPLANES-1) automatically get
// filled with 0 through this SAME loop, no extra special-casing needed
// (matches BlitFillRect's identical trick for the background).
procedure BuildSprite(spr : pointer of byte; wbytes, hgt, bscolor : byte);
var bp, bk, brow, bcount : integer;
	// BUG: bval is a 16-bit word, not a byte
	bval : byte; // Works
	//bval : integer; // Should work but doesn't

begin
	bcount := wbytes+2; // real content + 1 lookahead word, per plane per row
	for brow:=0 to hgt do begin
		for bp:=0 to DISPLAY_BITPLANES do begin
			if (((bscolor>>bp)&1)=1) then bval:=255 else bval:=0;
			for bk:=0 to bcount do begin
				spr[0] := bval;
				spr := spr+1;
			end;
		end;
	end;
end;

// Builds a single-bitplane mask for a debug box outline -- a 1px-thick
// hollow rectangle, not a solid block. mboxw is the true outline width in
// pixels, so one procedure can build entities of any box width (e.g. a
// 32px player/enemy box and a 43px attack box) from the same code. Built
// bit-by-bit (not byte-by-byte) since mboxw need not be byte-aligned
// (43 isn't).
//
// SIZING mwbytes (the caller's responsibility, not enforced here):
// mwbytes should be exactly ceil(mboxw/16) WORDS' worth of bytes -- i.e.
// round mboxw up to the nearest 16px and convert to bytes -- and NOT one
// word wider than that "just in case". It's tempting to also reserve an
// extra word here for the barrel shift's lookahead, but that word is
// already added separately, once, by whichever blit procedure reads this
// buffer (see BlitStampMasked's mbeffw := mwwords+1), so reserving it
// again here double-counts it and wastes about 25% of every sprite/mask
// buffer for nothing. This was gotten wrong once in this project's own
// history before being corrected -- the derivation, if it needs
// re-deriving: the box is drawn flush against the buffer's RIGHT edge (at
// mboxleft = mwbytes*8 - mboxw, computed below), and a blit with a
// lookahead word already appended is mbeffw=mwwords+1 words wide; shifting
// right by up to 15 puts the box's last bit at mwbytes*8 + 14 at most,
// which always fits inside that already-widened blit -- so mwbytes itself
// only ever needs to satisfy mwbytes*8 >= mboxw, nothing more.
//
// Empirically confirmed: the barrel shift delays/pushes content RIGHTWARD
// as shift_result rises from 0 to 15 -- so all the slack margin belongs on
// the LEFT, and the real box sits flush against the buffer's right edge
// (mwbytes*8, the NOMINAL width passed in here -- not the wider +1-word
// lookahead margin BlitStampMasked reserves internally on top of it).
// REWRITTEN for the interleaved-bitplane layout (see BuildSprite's own
// rewrite comment for the matching reasoning on the color/B-channel
// side). The mask is logically a SINGLE bitplane's worth of shape data,
// but since the destination screen buffer now walks DISPLAY_BITPLANES
// chunks per real row in ONE blitter operation (see BlitStampMasked's
// rewrite -- including the DISPLAY_BITPLANES-vs-NUM_PLANES padding-plane
// generalization), the mask data has to physically REPEAT once per
// plane-chunk of the WHOLE row (mrowbytes uses DISPLAY_BITPLANES), not
// just per real color plane. There's no cheaper way to make one channel
// re-read the same data once per plane without a modulo that varies
// mid-blit, which the hardware doesn't support (a channel's modulo is one
// fixed value for the whole operation). The content-drawing loop itself
// still only writes the REAL outline into the first NUM_PLANES chunks
// (mpl:=0 to NUM_PLANES) -- the padding chunks (NUM_PLANES..
// DISPLAY_BITPLANES-1) are left at 0 by the zero-init loop above, which
// is exactly what's wanted: mask=0 there makes BlitStampMasked's masked
// blit a no-op for those sub-rows (D=C, "leave background as-is"), so
// they never corrupt or need real content. Costs DISPLAY_BITPLANES times
// the mask memory instead of 1x -- trivial in absolute terms for buffers
// this small. No hidden padding ROW anymore either, same reasoning as
// BuildSprite: every chunk already carries its own genuine (not borrowed)
// lookahead word, so mhgt is the TRUE row count now, not row count+1.
procedure BuildBoxMask(mspr : pointer of byte; mwbytes, mhgt, mboxw : integer);
var mrow, mbit, mboxleft, mbyteoff, mzi, mchunk, mrowbytes, mpl : integer; mval : byte; mrp : pointer of byte;
begin
	mchunk := mwbytes+2;
	mrowbytes := mchunk*DISPLAY_BITPLANES;
	mrp := mspr;
	for mzi:=0 to mhgt*mrowbytes do begin
		mrp[0] := 0;
		mrp := mrp+1;
	end;

	mboxleft := mwbytes*8 - mboxw;

	for mrow:=0 to mhgt do begin
		for mbit:=0 to mboxw do begin
			mval := 0;
			if (mrow=0) then mval:=1;
			if (mrow=mhgt-1) then mval:=1;
			if (mbit=0) then mval:=1;
			if (mbit=mboxw-1) then mval:=1;
			if (mval=1) then begin
				for mpl:=0 to NUM_PLANES do begin
					mbyteoff := mrow*mrowbytes + mpl*mchunk + ((mboxleft+mbit)>>3);
					// Confirmed compiling to a safe "add.l" as of this
					// writing -- but see Quirk 3 EXTENDED (compiler-
					// quirks reference near the top of this file) before
					// touching this line: the identical "ptr := paramptr
					// + offsetvar" shape has been confirmed to ALSO
					// compile to a truncating "add.w" in other, textually
					// near-identical procedures. Re-verify against the
					// compiled assembly if this procedure is ever edited.
					mrp := mspr + mbyteoff;
					mrp[0] := mrp[0] | bittable[(mboxleft+mbit)&7];
				end;
			end;
		end;
	end;
end;

// Adds a SECOND, inset hollow-rectangle outline on top of an
// already-built mask -- does NOT clear the buffer first (unlike
// BuildBoxMask), so call BuildBoxMask for the outer border first, then
// this for the same mask buffer/dimensions plus a pixel inset. Used to
// give entities a distinct SHAPE instead of a distinct COLOR. Historical
// context: an earlier version of BlitStampMasked (see its own comment
// below for the root cause and fix) had a bug that corrupted the top rows
// of any entity whose color had a clear bitplane within this project's
// 3-bit (0-7) color budget, and was invisible only on color 7 (binary
// "111" -- no clear bitplane to corrupt). While diagnosing it, every
// entity was temporarily forced onto color 7 to rule color out as a
// factor -- which suppressed the corruption but also made all entities
// visually identical, so this inset ring was added as a second,
// color-independent way to tell them apart while that was true. The
// corruption itself was later root-caused and fixed for good, so distinct
// colors are safe again -- but the shape distinction was kept afterward
// as free, harmless insurance against this class of bug recurring (e.g.
// with future non-uniform sprite data). The
// outer silhouette (BuildBoxMask's own border) is left untouched, so the
// visible bounding box still faithfully matches the entity's real
// collision hitbox (this is a debug collision-box view -- CheckPlayer/
// EnemyAttackHits use their own hardcoded pixel offsets, not *_BOX_W, so
// this is purely cosmetic and never affects gameplay); only an inner ring
// is added as a decoration.
// REWRITTEN for the interleaved-bitplane layout -- same DISPLAY_BITPLANES-
// wide-layout/NUM_PLANES-real-content split as BuildBoxMask above (same
// mask buffer, same mchunk/mrowbytes-shaped layout, called AFTER
// BuildBoxMask on it), and same ihgt-is-now-the-true-row-count
// substitution (no more ireal_h).
procedure BuildBoxMaskInset(ispr : pointer of byte; iwbytes, ihgt, iboxw, iinset : integer);
var irow, ibit, iboxleft, ibyteoff, iw, ichunk, irowbytes, ipl : integer; ival : byte; irp : pointer of byte;
begin
	ichunk := iwbytes+2;
	irowbytes := ichunk*DISPLAY_BITPLANES;
	iboxleft := iwbytes*8 - iboxw;
	iw := iboxw - iinset*2;
	for irow:=iinset to ihgt-iinset do begin
		for ibit:=0 to iw do begin
			ival := 0;
			if (irow=iinset) then ival:=1;
			if (irow=ihgt-iinset-1) then ival:=1;
			if (ibit=0) then ival:=1;
			if (ibit=iw-1) then ival:=1;
			if (ival=1) then begin
				for ipl:=0 to NUM_PLANES do begin
					ibyteoff := irow*irowbytes + ipl*ichunk + ((iboxleft+iinset+ibit)>>3);
					// This is the original example Quirk 3 EXTENDED
					// (compiler-quirks reference near the top of this
					// file) was generalized from -- confirmed compiling
					// to a safe "add.l" as of this writing, but the SAME
					// shape has ALSO been confirmed to compile to a
					// truncating "add.w" elsewhere. Re-verify against the
					// compiled assembly if this procedure is ever edited.
					irp := ispr + ibyteoff;
					irp[0] := irp[0] | bittable[(iboxleft+iinset+ibit)&7];
				end;
			end;
		end;
	end;
end;

// ============================================================================
// Real image content + exact-color outlines -- the "framed bob" recipe.
// Moved here from tutorial.ras (where all three were prototyped and proven
// live in emulation) once they turned out to be genuinely reusable, not
// tutorial-specific: extracting a bob's sprite content from a background
// image instead of a flat BuildSprite fill, filling a solid INSET region of
// a mask (as opposed to BuildBoxMask's hollow border), and stamping an
// exact flat color onto a sprite buffer (as opposed to BuildBoxMaskInset's
// own OR-only approach, which can only ever brighten a color, never hold a
// plane at exactly 0 against arbitrary background content underneath).
// Together: a solid center of real image content, shown through a mask
// inset some margin in from the full box, surrounded by a transparent gap,
// with the original hollow-box outline drawn on top in a flat, exact color
// so it reads as a crisp line regardless of what's underneath it.
// ============================================================================

// Copies a cirwwords-word by cirhgt-row block, starting at pixel
// (cirsrcx, cirsrcy), from a tightly-packed NUM_PLANES-wide source image
// (this unit's own row-interleaved convention, no chunk padding -- 40
// bytes/plane/row, i.e. a 320px-wide source, matching the same assumption
// Bobs::CopyImageToScreen/Bobs::CaptureBgPristine already make) into a
// bob-shaped destination sprite buffer (padded per plane-chunk,
// (cirwwords+1)*2 bytes: real content + 1 lookahead word -- see
// Bobs::BuildSprite's own declaration comment for that layout). Lets a
// caller use REAL, non-uniform image content for a bob's sprite instead of
// Bobs::BuildSprite's uniform solid-color fill -- worth doing specifically
// because a solid fill is shift-invariant (any barrel-shift read position
// returns the same byte), so it can never expose BLTCON1's BSH field
// (channel B's own shift, separate from BLTCON0's ASH which shifts channel
// A/the mask only -- see Bobs::BlitStampMasked's own comment) being wrong
// or missing; real, varied content can, since a broken BSH would show up
// as the visible pattern smearing/misaligning as the bob crosses sub-pixel
// positions.
//
// Copies the FULL (cirwwords+1) words per row/plane, not just cirwwords --
// the extra word becomes the barrel shifter's own genuine lookahead margin
// (real continuing image content immediately to the right of the visible
// area, not zero-fill), matching exactly what Bobs::BlitStampMasked
// actually reads at runtime.
//
// One blitter operation per plane (NUM_PLANES total), not one for the
// whole buffer: source and destination need DIFFERENT per-row moduli (the
// source is tightly packed at ROW_STRIDE bytes/row across ALL its planes;
// the destination's own per-plane chunk is separated from the next row's
// SAME plane by the OTHER NUM_PLANES-1 chunks in between) -- so this can't
// use Bobs::BlitStampMasked's single-blit-spans-all-planes trick, which
// relies on source AND destination sharing the same "plane hop = row hop"
// symmetry that doesn't hold between these two, differently-shaped
// buffers.
//
// cirsrcx MUST be a multiple of 16 -- this is a plain 2-channel "D=A"
// blitter copy with no barrel shift, so the source pointer this procedure
// computes (cirsrcx>>3 bytes into the row) has to already land on a WORD
// boundary; the blitter's own address pointers are only well-defined on
// even (word-aligned) addresses. A cirsrcx that's a multiple of 8 but not
// 16 would compute an ODD byte offset -- an invalid blitter pointer, not
// merely "off by a pixel" -- and anything not even a multiple of 8 can't
// be expressed as a byte offset at all without a real barrel-shifted copy,
// which this procedure doesn't do. cirsrcy has no such constraint (whole
// rows only).
//
// cirsrcptr/cirdstptr are pointer VARIABLES (parameters), not "#symbol"
// literals. A plain TRSE-level "ptr := paramptr + precomputed_offset;"
// was tried first here, on the strength of Bobs::BuildBoxMaskInset's own
// "irp := ispr + ibyteoff" already using that exact shape safely -- but
// it turned out NOT to be reliable (see Quirk 3 EXTENDED, in the
// compiler-quirks reference near the top of this file, for the full
// story: the very same source shape compiled correctly in one of these
// procedures and silently truncated in two others). Every pointer combine
// below is hand-rolled in inline asm instead -- zero-extend the offset
// into a register, then "add.l" the pointer variable -- bypassing TRSE's
// own pointer-expression codegen entirely rather than trusting it to
// repeat whichever path it happened to take last time.
procedure CopyImageRegionToSprite(cirsrcptr, cirdstptr : pointer of byte; cirsrcx, cirsrcy, cirwwords, cirhgt : integer);
var cirpl, cirsmod, cirdmod, cirsize, cirsoff, cirdoff, cirxbytes, cirrowoff : integer; cirsrc, cirdst : pointer of byte;
begin
	cirxbytes := cirsrcx>>3;
	cirrowoff := cirsrcy*ROW_STRIDE;
	cirsmod := ROW_STRIDE - (cirwwords+1)*2;
	cirdmod := (cirwwords+1)*2*(NUM_PLANES-1);
	cirsize := (cirhgt<<6) | (cirwwords+1);
	for cirpl:=0 to NUM_PLANES do begin
		cirsoff := cirpl*40 + cirrowoff + cirxbytes;
		cirdoff := cirpl*((cirwwords+1)*2);
		asm("
			moveq #0,d0
			move.w Bobs_cirsoff,d0
			add.l Bobs_cirsrcptr,d0
			move.l d0,Bobs_cirsrc
			moveq #0,d0
			move.w Bobs_cirdoff,d0
			add.l Bobs_cirdstptr,d0
			move.l d0,Bobs_cirdst
		");
		asm("
			lea $dff000,a6
			move.l Bobs_cirsrc,a0
			move.l Bobs_cirdst,a1
		CopyImageRegionWait:
			btst #14,$dff002
			bne.s CopyImageRegionWait
			move.l #$ffffffff,BLTAFWM(a6)
			move.w #$09f0,BLTCON0(a6)
			move.w #$0,BLTCON1(a6)
			move.w Bobs_cirsmod,BLTAMOD(a6)
			move.w Bobs_cirdmod,BLTDMOD(a6)
			move.l a0,BLTAPTH(a6)
			move.l a1,BLTDPTH(a6)
			move.w Bobs_cirsize,BLTSIZE(a6)
		");
	end;
end;

// ORs a solid rectangle -- fsiboxw wide (minus fsiinset margin on each
// side), fsihgt tall (minus fsiinset margin top/bottom) -- into a mask
// buffer as opaque. The "solid center" half of the framed-bob recipe:
// call this INSTEAD of (or, as this section's own header explains, in
// ADDITION to, for the outline-on-top look) Bobs::BuildBoxMask's own
// hollow-border fill, to make an inset REGION of the box opaque rather
// than just its 1px outline.
//
// Bit-level, like Bobs::BuildBoxMask itself, not a byte fill -- a non-zero
// fsiinset generally starts and ends mid-byte, so this walks bit-by-bit
// the same way Bobs::BuildBoxMask does, just filling the WHOLE inner
// rectangle instead of only its border. Correctly accounts for
// fsiboxleft, the same flush-right placement Bobs::BuildBoxMask/
// Bobs::BuildBoxMaskInset both use (mboxleft/iboxleft there) -- needed for
// correctness whenever fsiboxw doesn't exactly fill fsiwbytes*8 (e.g. a
// caller sizing its sprite buffer the standard SPR_WORDS=ceil(BOX_W/16)
// way for a BOX_W that isn't itself a multiple of 16).
//
// Does NOT clear the buffer first (matching Bobs::BuildBoxMaskInset's own
// convention, not Bobs::BuildBoxMask's) -- call Bobs::BuildBoxMask first
// if the buffer needs a clean zero-init pass (its own comment explains
// why that matters: the lookahead byte and any DISPLAY_BITPLANES padding-
// plane chunks need a real zero, which this procedure never touches).
// ORs its own bits in, so it's safe to call before OR after
// Bobs::BuildBoxMaskInset on the same buffer -- OR is commutative,
// regardless of whether the two shapes' edges happen to coincide.
procedure FillSolidInsetMask(fsispr : pointer of byte; fsiwbytes, fsihgt, fsiboxw, fsiinset : integer);
var fsirow, fsibit, fsipl, fsibyteoff, fsichunk, fsirowbytes, fsiboxleft : integer; fsirp : pointer of byte;
begin
	fsichunk := fsiwbytes+2;
	fsirowbytes := fsichunk*NUM_PLANES;
	fsiboxleft := fsiwbytes*8 - fsiboxw;
	for fsirow:=fsiinset to fsihgt-fsiinset do begin
		for fsibit:=fsiinset to fsiboxw-fsiinset do begin
			for fsipl:=0 to NUM_PLANES do begin
				fsibyteoff := fsirow*fsirowbytes + fsipl*fsichunk + ((fsiboxleft+fsibit)>>3);
				asm("
					moveq #0,d0
					move.w Bobs_fsibyteoff,d0
					add.l Bobs_fsispr,d0
					move.l d0,Bobs_fsirp
				");
				fsirp[0] := fsirp[0] | bittable[(fsiboxleft+fsibit)&7];
			end;
		end;
	end;
end;

// Draws a hollow ring (the SAME shape/geometry Bobs::BuildBoxMaskInset
// draws -- dcinset=0 is the outer border, matching Bobs::BuildBoxMask's
// own outline, any larger dcinset an inset ring) onto a SPRITE (color)
// buffer in an EXACT flat color, not the OR-only approach
// Bobs::BuildBoxMaskInset itself uses. That distinction matters:
// Bobs::BuildBoxMaskInset can only ever SET bits, never CLEAR one that
// existing sprite content (e.g. from Bobs::CopyImageRegionToSprite above)
// already left set in some plane -- fine only for the one color whose
// every plane bit happens to already be 1 (NUM_PLANES color budget's own
// max value), wrong for any other color. This procedure sets dcolor's own
// bit per plane explicitly (OR where that plane's bit should be 1,
// AND-NOT where it should be 0), so the outline reads as EXACTLY dcolor
// regardless of what's underneath, for any dcolor.
//
// CHECK YOUR OWN PALETTE before picking dcolor -- don't assume a
// particular index is white (or any other specific color) without
// checking: this project's own tutorial got it backwards once, reading a
// COMPILED-IN DEFAULT/PLACEHOLDER copper palette in the static assembly
// rather than the REAL palette a caller's own setpalette() call loads at
// runtime from its actual source data -- confirm against the real
// palette bytes (or the runtime-loaded values), not the compiled binary's
// own placeholder defaults, which are never what actually gets displayed
// once setpalette() overwrites them.
//
// Call this AFTER filling the sprite's own background content (e.g.
// Bobs::CopyImageRegionToSprite above) -- these rings are meant to be
// drawn "on top", so they read as a crisp, exact color regardless of what
// content is underneath, not the other way around.
//
// BBUSY WAIT, FIRST THING, before any of the writes below -- this
// procedure is pure CPU (plain byte writes, no blitter operation of its
// own), and every blit-triggering procedure in this unit waits for BBUSY
// BEFORE its own next blit, never AFTER its own last one (e.g.
// Bobs::CopyImageRegionToSprite above, whose own last blit is triggered
// and left running when it returns) -- safe only as long as whatever
// runs next is itself blitter-aware, so its own leading BBUSY check
// naturally serializes against the still-running blit. A caller of THIS
// procedure right after a blit-triggering one (its own most likely use,
// per this section's own header) would break that assumption without
// this wait: it would start writing over the SAME buffer the blitter
// might still be mid-DMA on. Checking here, rather than leaving it to
// every future caller to remember, means this procedure is safe to call
// immediately after any blit-triggering one regardless.
procedure DrawColorRingOnSprite(dcspr : pointer of byte; dcwbytes, dchgt, dcboxw, dcinset, dcolor : integer);
var dcrow, dcbit, dcpl, dcw, dcboxleft, dcval, dcbyteoff, dcplbit, dcbitidx, dcchunk, dcrowbytes : integer; dcrp : pointer of byte;
begin
	asm("
		DrawColorRingBlitWait:
		btst #14,$dff002
		bne.s DrawColorRingBlitWait
	");
	dcchunk := dcwbytes+2;
	dcrowbytes := dcchunk*NUM_PLANES;
	dcboxleft := dcwbytes*8 - dcboxw;
	dcw := dcboxw - dcinset*2;
	for dcrow:=dcinset to dchgt-dcinset do begin
		for dcbit:=0 to dcw do begin
			dcval := 0;
			if (dcrow=dcinset) then dcval:=1;
			if (dcrow=dchgt-dcinset-1) then dcval:=1;
			if (dcbit=0) then dcval:=1;
			if (dcbit=dcw-1) then dcval:=1;
			if (dcval=1) then begin
				dcbitidx := (dcboxleft+dcinset+dcbit)&7;
				for dcpl:=0 to NUM_PLANES do begin
					dcbyteoff := dcrow*dcrowbytes + dcpl*dcchunk + ((dcboxleft+dcinset+dcbit)>>3);
					asm("
						moveq #0,d0
						move.w Bobs_dcbyteoff,d0
						add.l Bobs_dcspr,d0
						move.l d0,Bobs_dcrp
					");
					dcplbit := (dcolor>>dcpl)&1;
					if (dcplbit=1) then dcrp[0] := dcrp[0] | bittable[dcbitidx]
					else dcrp[0] := dcrp[0] & (255-bittable[dcbitidx]);
				end;
			end;
		end;
	end;
end;

// Masked blit: real (non-rectangular) sprite drawing, combined with the
// barrel shifter for true per-pixel x-positioning. Uses all four
// blitter channels -- A = mask, B = color data, C = destination readback
// (so the blitter can see what is already on screen), D = destination
// write. Minterm $CA implements D = (A AND B) OR (NOT A AND C): where the
// mask is opaque (1), take the sprite's own color bit; where it's
// transparent (0), keep whatever background was already there -- the
// standard Amiga "cookie cutter" masked-sprite technique. ASH (in mcon0)
// and BSH (in mcon1) shift A and B together, so mask and image stay
// pixel-aligned as they move.
//
// REWRITTEN for the interleaved-bitplane layout: this used to loop 3
// times (once per plane), each iteration a full BLTSIZE-triggered
// blitter operation with its own BBUSY wait and register reload. Now
// it's ONE blitter operation for the WHOLE multi-plane entity -- this is
// the actual point of the interleaved rewrite. It follows up on an
// earlier experiment with a synthetic "wide enemy" test entity: 3x the
// normal width but touching only 1 bitplane instead of 3, so a single
// instance's one blitter operation moved exactly as much data as 3
// normal (3-plane) entities combined. Comparing "3x data, 1 operation"
// against "1x data, 3 operations" showed the former reliably supports
// more simultaneous on-screen objects for the same total bytes moved --
// confirming the win comes from fewer per-operation setups (register
// pokes, the BBUSY wait, procedure-call overhead), not from the
// underlying data-movement cost, which is fixed per word regardless of
// layout. This rewrite generalizes that same win to the real gameplay
// entities instead of a synthetic test one.
//
// Mechanism: BLTSIZE's height field now counts mwhgt*DISPLAY_BITPLANES
// "sub-rows" -- NOT mwhgt*NUM_PLANES -- each sub-row is exactly one
// plane's worth of one real row (mbeffw words wide, the same "+1
// lookahead word" margin as before). Walking forward by mcdmod bytes
// after every sub-row on the C/D (screen) side ALWAYS lands on the same
// destination column, whether the next sub-row is the next plane of the
// SAME real row or plane 0 of the NEXT real row -- both are just "skip to
// the start of the next 40-byte block" hops in this layout, since planes
// are stored back-to-back per row with no gaps (see NUM_PLANES/
// ROW_STRIDE's own comments). That symmetry is what makes a single fixed
// modulo correct across the whole multi-plane, multi-row operation, with
// the SAME mcdmod formula (40 - mbeffw*2) this procedure already used
// per-plane before the rewrite -- BUT it only holds if the blit walks
// through EVERY block the real row actually contains, including
// DISPLAY_BITPLANES's "extra" padding planes beyond our own NUM_PLANES
// color budget; stopping at NUM_PLANES sub-rows per row would jump
// straight into the middle of the padding planes instead of the next
// row's plane 0 the moment DISPLAY_BITPLANES > NUM_PLANES. The mask (A)
// channel being 0 for those padding sub-rows (see BuildBoxMask's own
// rewrite comment) is what makes walking through them harmless: D=(A AND
// B) OR (NOT A AND C) with A=0 reduces to D=C, a no-op that leaves
// whatever's already in that (always-zero, never-drawn-to) padding plane
// untouched.
//
// The mask (A) and sprite (B) source buffers are built to match this
// same per-sub-row cadence directly (see BuildSprite/BuildBoxMask's own
// rewrite comments) -- both are walked with modulo 0 (tightly packed),
// no AMOD/BMOD rewind trick needed at all anymore: every sub-row already
// carries its own genuine lookahead word (not borrowed from a neighbour,
// which would be WRONG here since neighbouring sub-rows can be different
// PLANES with different fill values -- unlike the old true-planar
// version, where "the next row" always meant the next row of the SAME
// plane, making the borrow safe). There's no more "true last row" special
// case either, since nothing borrows across a boundary in the first
// place.
//
// Historical note on why this procedure always uses the wide (mbeffw =
// mwwords+1) buffer width and the modulo-0 tightly-packed reads above,
// with no shortcut for mdshift=0: an earlier, pre-rewrite version of this
// same masked+shifted blit special-cased mdshift=0 as an optimization --
// "no shift means no lookahead word needed" -- using a narrower buffer
// width and no modulo rewind. That shortcut is safe for a plain 2-channel
// copy (still used by the restore procedures below, at shift=0 always),
// but was NOT safe for this 4-channel cookie-cut blit: it corrupted the
// top rows of the sprite every time an entity's x-position crossed an
// exact 16-pixel boundary (mdshift naturally hits exactly 0 there during
// ordinary movement, not just at screen edges). Root-caused by forcing
// the shift value to specific literals one at a time and observing that
// every value which took the "assume real shifting" (wide/rewind) code
// path rendered cleanly, while only the value that took the narrow/
// no-rewind shortcut glitched -- confirming the shortcut itself, not the
// shift value, was the bug. Fixed by removing the special case entirely.
// This rewritten, single-blit design has no narrow/wide shortcut branch
// left at all to accidentally reintroduce that class of bug.
//
// draw_target holds #screen or #screen2 (see its own declaration comment
// above) -- reading/writing it as "Bobs_draw_target" inside the asm block
// below per this unit's own cross-file rules (see this file's header
// comment). Its use here as a pointer VARIABLE, rather than a "#symbol",
// is exactly Quirk 3 in the compiler-quirks reference near the top of
// this file: precompute the offset as a plain integer (mdstoff below),
// then hand-roll the final pointer add in asm with an explicit add.l.
procedure BlitStampMasked(mdst_bx, mdst_by, mwwords, mwhgt, mdshift : integer; wmask, mwspr : pointer of byte);
var mcdmod, msize, mcon0, mcon1, mbeffw, mdstoff : integer;
var masrc, mbsrc, mcdst, mddst : pointer of byte;
begin
	mbeffw := mwwords+1;
	mcdmod := 40 - mbeffw*2;
	msize := ((mwhgt*DISPLAY_BITPLANES)<<6) | mbeffw;
	mcon0 := ((mdshift << 8) << 4) | $0FCA;
	// BSH (channel B's own shift field, BLTCON1 bits 15-12) must be set
	// alongside ASH (mcon0's own shift field, which only shifts channel
	// A/the mask) -- otherwise channel B's color data stays word-aligned
	// while the mask moves to the true sub-pixel position, desyncing mask
	// and color data by up to 15px. This is invisible whenever the sprite
	// buffer is filled with a uniform value throughout (as BuildSprite's
	// solid-color fill does today) -- any read position within a uniform
	// buffer gives the same value regardless of shift -- but it becomes a
	// real, visible bug the moment non-uniform (real pixel-art) sprite
	// data replaces a uniform placeholder fill.
	mcon1 := (mdshift << 8) << 4;
	masrc := wmask;
	mbsrc := mwspr;
	mdstoff := mdst_by*ROW_STRIDE + mdst_bx;
	asm("
		move.l Bobs_draw_target,d0
		moveq #0,d1
		move.w Bobs_mdstoff,d1
		add.l d1,d0
		move.l d0,Bobs_mcdst
	");
	mddst := mcdst;
	asm("
		lea $dff000,a6
		move.l Bobs_masrc,a0
		move.l Bobs_mbsrc,a1
		move.l Bobs_mcdst,a2
		move.l Bobs_mddst,a3
	MaskBlitWait:
		btst #14,$dff002
		bne.s MaskBlitWait
		move.l #$ffffffff,BLTAFWM(a6)
		move.w Bobs_mcon0,BLTCON0(a6)
		move.w Bobs_mcon1,BLTCON1(a6)
		move.w #$0,BLTAMOD(a6)
		move.w #$0,BLTBMOD(a6)
		move.w Bobs_mcdmod,BLTCMOD(a6)
		move.w Bobs_mcdmod,BLTDMOD(a6)
		move.l a0,BLTAPTH(a6)
		move.l a1,BLTBPTH(a6)
		move.l a2,BLTCPTH(a6)
		move.l a3,BLTDPTH(a6)
		move.w Bobs_msize,BLTSIZE(a6)
	");
end;

// ALTERNATE entity draw. This unit doesn't dispatch between this and
// BlitStampMasked itself -- callers pick whichever one they want at each
// call site, e.g. via their own dispatch flag declared as a plain
// variable rather than a "const" (see Quirk 4 in the compiler-quirks
// reference near the top of this file for why a const-based mode switch
// is unsafe). Reach for this procedure instead of BlitStampMasked above
// when
// DISPLAY_BITPLANES > NUM_PLANES is actually wanted for real (more total
// on-screen colors, not just a quick DMA-cost benchmark): BlitStampMasked
// is fast BUT pays for walking DISPLAY_BITPLANES sub-rows per real row,
// including the always-empty padding ones (needed to keep its single
// fixed modulo valid across both plane and row boundaries -- see its own
// comment). This procedure instead does NUM_PLANES SEPARATE blitter
// operations, one per REAL plane, each spanning all mwhgt rows with its
// OWN modulo (ROW_STRIDE - mbeffw*2) that skips straight from one row's
// plane p to the NEXT row's SAME plane p, jumping over however many other
// planes (real or padding) sit in between in a single hop -- indifferent
// to DISPLAY_BITPLANES's value, since ROW_STRIDE already accounts for it.
// This is structurally the SAME technique BlitStamp/the old true-planar
// BlitStampMasked always used, just with ROW_STRIDE-relative addressing
// instead of PLANE_SIZE-relative. Reads the SAME mask/sprite buffers as
// BlitStampMasked (no separate buffers needed): both were already laid
// out with DISPLAY_BITPLANES chunks per row (see BuildSprite/BuildBoxMask),
// so picking out just plane mpl's chunk from each row and skipping the
// rest is exactly what this procedure's own AMOD/BMOD (mrowbytes-mchunk)
// does. Costs NUM_PLANES separate BBUSY-wait+register-set+trigger
// sequences per entity (matching this project's ORIGINAL pre-single-blit
// cost) -- slower than BlitStampMasked at DISPLAY_BITPLANES=NUM_PLANES,
// which is exactly why it's not the default; but its total blitter data
// moved per entity is INDEPENDENT of DISPLAY_BITPLANES, unlike
// BlitStampMasked's, so it's the one to reach for once DISPLAY_BITPLANES
// is being raised for real extra on-screen colors rather than as a
// temporary DMA-cost probe.
// NOTE: parameters are all "pp"-prefixed and distinct from BlitStampMasked's
// own (mdst_bx/mdst_by/etc.). TRSE gives every identifier in a program a
// single fixed memory address -- there is no real per-procedure scoping,
// only a parse-time check for outright duplicate declarations -- so a
// PARAMETER name is just as global as a top-level variable name, and every
// identifier in the whole program (loop counters, parameter names, locals)
// must be unique. Reusing BlitStampMasked's exact parameter names here
// failed the build outright ("Variable 'mdst_bx' is already defined!"),
// not just a style choice.
procedure BlitStampMaskedPerPlane(ppdst_bx, ppdst_by, ppwwords, ppwhgt, ppdshift : integer; ppwmask, ppwspr : pointer of byte);
var ppabmod, ppcdmod, ppsize, pprowoff, ppcon0, ppcon1, ppbeffw, ppchunk, pprowbytes, ppl, ppdstoff : integer;
var ppasrc, ppbsrc, ppcdst, ppddst : pointer of byte;
begin
	ppbeffw := ppwwords+1;
	ppchunk := ppbeffw*2;
	pprowbytes := ppchunk*DISPLAY_BITPLANES;
	// A/B (mask/sprite) modulo: skip the OTHER DISPLAY_BITPLANES-1 chunks
	// in this row to land on the SAME plane's chunk in the next row --
	// same "whole row stride minus this chunk's own width" shape as the
	// C/D modulo below, just measured against the mask/sprite buffer's
	// own row width (pprowbytes) instead of the real screen's (ROW_STRIDE).
	ppabmod := pprowbytes - ppchunk;
	ppcdmod := ROW_STRIDE - ppchunk;
	ppsize := (ppwhgt<<6) | ppbeffw;
	ppcon0 := ((ppdshift << 8) << 4) | $0FCA;
	ppcon1 := (ppdshift << 8) << 4;
	// Precomputed once, reused per plane below -- can't just be inlined as
	// a 3rd term alongside "ppl*40" (that would be two inline
	// multiplications in one pointer expression, exactly the shape Quirk 1
	// in the compiler-quirks reference near the top of this file confirms
	// unsafe).
	pprowoff := ppdst_by*ROW_STRIDE + ppdst_bx;
	for ppl:=0 to NUM_PLANES do begin
		// "ppwmask + ppl*ppchunk" -- a 2-term expression, base plus ONE
		// inline multiplication, nothing else -- matches the proven-safe
		// 2-term "BASE + mult" shape (e.g. CaptureBgPristine's own
		// "cscreen + 40*cppl", further down in this same file).
		ppasrc := ppwmask + ppl*ppchunk;
		ppbsrc := ppwspr + ppl*ppchunk;
		ppdstoff := ppl*40 + pprowoff;
		asm("
			move.l Bobs_draw_target,d0
			moveq #0,d1
			move.w Bobs_ppdstoff,d1
			add.l d1,d0
			move.l d0,Bobs_ppcdst
		");
		ppddst := ppcdst;
		asm("
			lea $dff000,a6
			move.l Bobs_ppasrc,a0
			move.l Bobs_ppbsrc,a1
			move.l Bobs_ppcdst,a2
			move.l Bobs_ppddst,a3
		MaskBlitPPWait:
			btst #14,$dff002
			bne.s MaskBlitPPWait
			move.l #$ffffffff,BLTAFWM(a6)
			move.w Bobs_ppcon0,BLTCON0(a6)
			move.w Bobs_ppcon1,BLTCON1(a6)
			move.w Bobs_ppabmod,BLTAMOD(a6)
			move.w Bobs_ppabmod,BLTBMOD(a6)
			move.w Bobs_ppcdmod,BLTCMOD(a6)
			move.w Bobs_ppcdmod,BLTDMOD(a6)
			move.l a0,BLTAPTH(a6)
			move.l a1,BLTBPTH(a6)
			move.l a2,BLTCPTH(a6)
			move.l a3,BLTDPTH(a6)
			move.w Bobs_ppsize,BLTSIZE(a6)
		");
	end;
end;

// Restores a rectangle directly from bg_pristine to the screen -- both
// sides are "screen-shaped" (real ROW_STRIDE-wide rows), so BOTH channels
// need the SAME modulo here. This is what lets the per-frame Save phase
// stay gone by default: bg_pristine is always correct for any position,
// so there's nothing to snapshot each frame -- restore just pulls
// straight from it using the entity's OLD position, same as always.
// Always shift=0 (restoring wants back exactly the true background, no
// repositioning), so no beffw/BLTAMOD-rewind branching is needed at all.
//
// REWRITTEN for the interleaved-bitplane layout -- same "one blit instead
// of nplanes" mechanism as BlitStampMasked's own rewrite (see its comment
// for the full reasoning), but simpler here since restoring is a plain
// 2-channel "D=A" copy (no mask/shift), and both sides (screen and
// bg_pristine) share the IDENTICAL interleaved layout, so both use the
// SAME modulo. Call sites pass nplanes=DISPLAY_BITPLANES (not a hardcoded
// 3) -- same DISPLAY_BITPLANES-vs-NUM_PLANES padding-plane reasoning as
// BlitStampMasked: restore must walk through every plane the masked
// draw's single blit did, real or padding, for the row-vs-plane modulo
// symmetry to hold. nplanes is now a height MULTIPLIER instead of a loop
// bound, but the parameter's job (how many plane-chunks per real row)
// means exactly the same thing either way.
procedure RestoreFromPristine(dst_bx, dst_by, wwords, whgt, nplanes : integer);
var prmod, prsize, prdstoff : integer; prsrc, prdst : pointer of byte;
begin
	prmod := 40 - wwords*2;
	prsize := ((whgt*nplanes)<<6) | wwords;
	// #bg_pristine is now this unit's own array (see this file's header
	// comment) -- referenced bare, no "Bobs_" prefix needed, since this is
	// plain Pascal-level code (not raw asm) inside the SAME unit that owns
	// it.
	prsrc := #bg_pristine + dst_by*ROW_STRIDE + dst_bx;
	prdstoff := dst_by*ROW_STRIDE + dst_bx;
	asm("
		move.l Bobs_draw_target,d0
		moveq #0,d1
		move.w Bobs_prdstoff,d1
		add.l d1,d0
		move.l d0,Bobs_prdst
	");
	asm("
		lea $dff000,a6
		move.l Bobs_prsrc,a0
		move.l Bobs_prdst,a1
	RestorePristineWait:
		btst #14,$dff002
		bne.s RestorePristineWait
		move.l #$ffffffff,BLTAFWM(a6)
		move.w #$09f0,BLTCON0(a6)
		move.w #$0,BLTCON1(a6)
		move.w Bobs_prmod,BLTAMOD(a6)
		move.w Bobs_prmod,BLTDMOD(a6)
		move.l a0,BLTAPTH(a6)
		move.l a1,BLTDPTH(a6)
		move.w Bobs_prsize,BLTSIZE(a6)
	");
end;

// ALTERNATE restore, paired with BlitStampMaskedPerPlane above -- selected
// the same way, via ENTITY_BLIT_MODE. Same reasoning as that procedure:
// NUM_PLANES separate 2-channel copies (one per REAL plane), each using
// modulo (ROW_STRIDE - wwords*2) to skip straight to the next row of the
// SAME plane, indifferent to how many padding planes DISPLAY_BITPLANES
// adds in between. No "nplanes" parameter needed (unlike
// RestoreFromPristine) since it's always exactly NUM_PLANES separate
// operations here, never DISPLAY_BITPLANES-many.
// NOTE: parameters are "rpp"-prefixed, distinct from RestoreFromPristine's
// own (dst_bx/dst_by/wwords/whgt) -- see BlitStampMaskedPerPlane's own
// comment: TRSE doesn't scope parameter names per procedure, so reusing
// those exact names here fails the build the same way theirs did.
procedure RestoreFromPristinePerPlane(rppdst_bx, rppdst_by, rppwwords, rppwhgt : integer);
var rppmod, rppsize, rprowoff, rppl, rppdstoff : integer; rppsrc, rppdst : pointer of byte;
begin
	rppmod := ROW_STRIDE - rppwwords*2;
	rppsize := (rppwhgt<<6) | rppwwords;
	rprowoff := rppdst_by*ROW_STRIDE + rppdst_bx;
	for rppl:=0 to NUM_PLANES do begin
		rppsrc := #bg_pristine + rppl*40 + rprowoff;
		rppdstoff := rppl*40 + rprowoff;
		asm("
			move.l Bobs_draw_target,d0
			moveq #0,d1
			move.w Bobs_rppdstoff,d1
			add.l d1,d0
			move.l d0,Bobs_rppdst
		");
		asm("
			lea $dff000,a6
			move.l Bobs_rppsrc,a0
			move.l Bobs_rppdst,a1
		RestorePristinePPWait:
			btst #14,$dff002
			bne.s RestorePristinePPWait
			move.l #$ffffffff,BLTAFWM(a6)
			move.w #$09f0,BLTCON0(a6)
			move.w #$0,BLTCON1(a6)
			move.w Bobs_rppmod,BLTAMOD(a6)
			move.w Bobs_rppmod,BLTDMOD(a6)
			move.l a0,BLTAPTH(a6)
			move.l a1,BLTDPTH(a6)
			move.w Bobs_rppsize,BLTSIZE(a6)
		");
	end;
end;

// ============================================================================
// Automatic dirty-rect tracking -- EXPERIMENTAL, OPT-IN, ADDITIVE ONLY.
//
// UNTESTED IN EMULATION as of this writing: confirmed to compile cleanly
// (headless CLI build) and every call site below was hand-checked against
// its callee's real parameter order/count, but neither main.ras nor
// tutorial.ras calls any procedure in this section, so nothing has
// actually exercised it against real screen/blitter state yet. Wire it
// into a real caller (see the usage sketch below) and confirm bobs drawn
// this way actually erase correctly frame to frame before relying on it
// for anything real.
//
// Every draw/restore procedure above (BlitStampMasked, RestoreFromPristine,
// etc.) is UNCHANGED by this section -- nothing here alters their behavior
// or signature. This is a second, parallel way to drive them: instead of a
// caller hand-maintaining its own per-buffer position history (the
// buf_bx/buf_by/buf_b_alive-shaped arrays main.ras and tutorial.ras each
// declare and update themselves, one entry per bob per buffer, plus their
// own SaveBufferPositions-style procedure), this unit can track "what got
// drawn into which buffer, and where" automatically, and hand it straight
// back for restoring -- no caller-side bookkeeping, no fixed-size per-bob
// history arrays.
//
// MECHANISM: two per-buffer lists (buf id 0 and 1, matching the convention
// every double-buffering piece in this unit already uses), each a plain
// fixed-capacity array acting as a growable list at runtime -- TRSE has no
// heap/dynamic allocation to reach for here, so DIRTY_MAX (see its own
// declaration comment above) is a hard cap, not a soft one; see "known
// limitations" below. Drawing a bob via BlitStampMaskedTracked (instead of
// calling BlitStampMasked directly) both draws it AND appends its
// destination rectangle to that buffer's list. RestoreDirty(buf id) walks
// the WHOLE list for that buffer, restores every rectangle in it from
// bg_pristine, then empties the list -- so the next frame's draws start
// building a fresh list from nothing. Under double buffering, that means:
// RestoreDirty at the top of a frame always restores exactly what THIS
// SAME buffer had drawn on it the last time it was the back buffer (two
// frames ago), because nothing else clears or touches the list in between.
//
// USAGE SKETCH -- replaces a caller's own Restore/Update/Draw/Save block.
// Neither main.ras nor tutorial.ras has been converted to this; both keep
// their existing hand-rolled history arrays unchanged for now, per the
// "old functions stay as they are" intent behind keeping this additive:
//   interrupt VBlank();
//   begin
//       Bobs::RestoreDirty(Bobs_cur_buf);   // undo last frame's draws into THIS buffer
//       UpdateBobs();
//       for i:=0 to NUM_BOBS do begin
//           Bobs::ClampWordCol(bx[i]-BOB_BOX_LEFT, (BOB_SPR_WORDS+1)*2);
//           Bobs::BlitStampMaskedTracked(Bobs_cur_buf, Bobs_blk_result, by[i]-64,
//               BOB_SPR_WORDS, BOB_SPR_H, Bobs_shift_result, #bob_mask, #bob_sprite);
//       end;
//       Bobs::FlipBuffer(#screen, #screen2);
//   end;
// No buf_bx/buf_by/buf_b_alive arrays, no SaveBufferPositions call, no
// per-bob "which buffer, which slot" indexing -- the list already knows.
//
// WHY THE OLD (MANUAL-HISTORY) APPROACH STAYS THE DEFAULT, NOT REPLACED:
// this trades a small, fixed amount of per-bob overhead (two array writes
// per draw call to append; a loop plus an extra procedure-call layer per
// restored rectangle, instead of the caller's own tight, already-open-
// coded loop) for not having to hand-maintain position history at all.
// Whether that trade is worth it depends on how many bobs a given caller
// draws per frame and how tight its own frame budget already is -- exactly
// the kind of thing this project's own history of bandwidth-vs-overhead
// measurements says needs measuring on real hardware/emulation, not
// assumed. Both mechanisms coexist so that trade can actually be measured,
// caller by caller, rather than picked once for everyone. NOT YET
// MEASURED as of this writing.
//
// LIMITATIONS of this first pass, deliberately not addressed yet:
// - Covers only the default single-blit path (BlitStampMasked/
//   RestoreFromPristine). BlitStampMaskedPerPlane/
//   RestoreFromPristinePerPlane (the ENTITY_BLIT_MODE=1-equivalent
//   alternate technique) have no "*Tracked" counterpart yet -- add one the
//   same way (call the PerPlane draw, then MarkDirty) if that path needs
//   this too.
// - DIRTY_MAX is a hard per-buffer cap, checked at MarkDirty time
//   (silently drops the record if already full -- see its own comment). A
//   caller that draws more than DIRTY_MAX bobs into one buffer in a single
//   frame will have some of them never auto-restored. Bump DIRTY_MAX (its
//   declaration, near the top of this file) if that happens; there is no
//   dynamic growth to fall back on.
// - No de-duplication or merging: drawing the same bob (or overlapping
//   bobs) more than once before the next RestoreDirty records -- and later
//   restores -- every individual rectangle, even if they overlap. Harmless
//   (restoring the same pixels twice is a no-op, just wasted blitter
//   cycles), not incorrect.
// ============================================================================

// Appends one destination rectangle to mdbuf's dirty list. Called by
// BlitStampMaskedTracked below right after drawing -- not usually called
// directly, but exposed in case a caller wants to draw with plain
// BlitStampMasked (e.g. to reuse an existing call site unchanged) and only
// add tracking as a separate second step.
//
// Silently does nothing once dirty_count[mdbuf] reaches DIRTY_MAX, rather
// than overflowing the array -- see this section's own "known
// limitations" note above. The comparison is against a positive constant
// (DIRTY_MAX) and a runtime array read, not literal 0 on either side, so
// this isn't the unsigned-branch shape Quirk 2 (compiler-quirks reference
// near the top of this file) warns about.
procedure MarkDirty(mdbuf, mdblk, mdy, mdww, mdwh, mdnp : integer);
var mdslot : integer;
begin
	if (dirty_count[mdbuf] < DIRTY_MAX) then begin
		mdslot := mdbuf*DIRTY_MAX + dirty_count[mdbuf];
		dirty_blk[mdslot] := mdblk;
		dirty_y[mdslot] := mdy;
		dirty_ww[mdslot] := mdww;
		dirty_wh[mdslot] := mdwh;
		dirty_np[mdslot] := mdnp;
		dirty_count[mdbuf] := dirty_count[mdbuf] + 1;
	end;
end;

// Draws exactly as BlitStampMasked does (calls it directly, unchanged,
// with the SAME tkwwords a direct BlitStampMasked call would take), then
// records the drawn rectangle in tkbuf's dirty list via MarkDirty -- the
// "automatic" half of this section's mechanism. DISPLAY_BITPLANES is
// passed to MarkDirty as the plane count, matching what RestoreFromPristine's
// own nplanes parameter expects when restoring this same rectangle later
// (see BlitStampMasked's own comment for why the draw and its later
// restore must walk the same plane count).
//
// WIDTH NOTE, easy to get wrong and worth spelling out: BlitStampMasked
// widens tkwwords by one word internally for its own blit (mbeffw :=
// mwwords+1, its own comment above has the full reasoning), but
// RestoreFromPristine does NOT do any such widening itself -- its own
// wwords parameter is used as the literal blit width, so a caller of
// RestoreFromPristine directly has always had to pass the ALREADY-widened
// value (e.g. this file's own tutorial usage sketch passes BOB_SPR_WORDS+1,
// not BOB_SPR_WORDS). MarkDirty therefore records tkwwords+1 here, not
// tkwwords -- recording the raw, unwidened value would make RestoreDirty's
// later restore one word narrower than the actual drawn footprint,
// leaving an unerased sliver at the shifted edge nearly every frame. This
// was caught by hand-deriving it against RestoreFromPristine's own
// (whgt*nplanes)<<6 | wwords line, not by symptom -- if this mechanism is
// ever extended (e.g. a *PerPlane tracked variant), re-derive the correct
// width the same way rather than assuming symmetry with the draw side.
// NOTE: parameters are "tk"-prefixed, distinct from BlitStampMasked's own
// (mdst_bx/etc.) and from MarkDirty's own (mdbuf/etc.) -- TRSE doesn't
// scope identifiers per procedure (see BlitStampMaskedPerPlane's own
// comment for the full reasoning), so every procedure in this file needs
// its own distinct parameter names.
procedure BlitStampMaskedTracked(tkbuf, tkdst_bx, tkdst_by, tkwwords, tkwhgt, tkdshift : integer; tkwmask, tkwspr : pointer of byte);
begin
	BlitStampMasked(tkdst_bx, tkdst_by, tkwwords, tkwhgt, tkdshift, tkwmask, tkwspr);
	MarkDirty(tkbuf, tkdst_bx, tkdst_by, tkwwords+1, tkwhgt, DISPLAY_BITPLANES);
end;

// Restores every rectangle recorded in rdbuf's dirty list (via
// RestoreFromPristine, one call per rectangle), then empties the list --
// this IS the "cleared out on the next buffer restore" behavior: once
// this returns, rdbuf's list has nothing in it until this frame's own
// BlitStampMaskedTracked calls repopulate it.
//
// ⚠ Guarded with an explicit tst.w/beq skip around the whole for loop --
// see Quirk 6 in the compiler-quirks reference near the top of this file.
// Without the guard, an empty list (rdn=0, the normal case on a buffer's
// first-ever use) still ran the loop body ONCE with garbage data (nothing
// was ever written to slot 0), then looped for up to 65536 iterations
// before a 16-bit wraparound accidentally let it exit -- each iteration a
// real blitter operation at a garbage address. Symptom, confirmed by
// direct assembly inspection after the fact rather than guessed: clean
// background for a few seconds (however long ~65536 blitter round-trips
// takes), then rapidly escalating full-screen corruption, with the
// tracked bobs never visibly drawn at all -- VBlank never got past this,
// its very first statement, on its very first call.
procedure RestoreDirty(rdbuf : integer);
var rdslot, rdi, rdn : integer;
begin
	rdn := dirty_count[rdbuf];
	asm("
		tst.w Bobs_rdn
		beq RestoreDirtyEmpty
	");
	for rdi:=0 to rdn do begin
		rdslot := rdbuf*DIRTY_MAX + rdi;
		RestoreFromPristine(dirty_blk[rdslot], dirty_y[rdslot], dirty_ww[rdslot], dirty_wh[rdslot], dirty_np[rdslot]);
	end;
	asm("
	RestoreDirtyEmpty:
	");
	dirty_count[rdbuf] := 0;
end;

// ============================================================================
// Tier 2 -- screen/DMA infrastructure closely tied to the bob mechanism
// (moved from main.ras). #screen/#screen2 (and, for CopyImageToScreen
// below, the caller's own source image) are passed in as parameters rather
// than reached for by plain name, since this unit can't reach a plain-
// named global declared elsewhere (rule 1 at the top of this file).
//
// FlipBuffer itself has TWO homes, deliberately: a fully self-contained
// version lives HERE (own cur_buf, see its declaration comment, takes
// #screen/#screen2 as parameters -- see tutorial.ras for the intended
// "just call Bobs::FlipBuffer(#screen, #screen2) every frame" usage), but
// main.ras (the larger project this unit was extracted from) keeps its
// OWN separate FlipBuffer with its OWN cur_buf, since that one also has to
// index player/attack position history that has nothing to do with
// drawing a bob and would drag unrelated game-state bookkeeping in here if
// unified. Both versions ultimately just call FlipToBuffer below and write
// draw_target -- picking a caller's own FlipBuffer vs. this unit's is a
// judgment call based on how much OTHER per-buffer state that caller has
// to keep in sync at the same time.
// ============================================================================

// One-time whole-screen copy of a tightly-packed, NUM_PLANES-wide source
// image (e.g. one exported via TRSE's own image tools, matching this
// project's color budget but carrying no DISPLAY_BITPLANES padding) into a
// screen-shaped (ROW_STRIDE-wide) destination -- see tutorial.ras's own
// `image` declaration comment for why the source and destination need
// DIFFERENT moduli here (cipsmod for the tightly-packed source, cipdmod
// for the padded destination), unlike CopyScreenToScreen2 below, where
// both sides share the same layout and modulo.
//
// Per-plane loop, same reasoning as CaptureBgPristine/CopyScreenToScreen2:
// a single whole-buffer BLTSIZE call would need a width of ROW_STRIDE/2
// words, which overflows BLTSIZE's 6-bit width field once ROW_STRIDE
// exceeds 126 bytes -- safe today but not worth relying on, and this is a
// one-time startup cost regardless.
procedure CopyImageToScreen(cisrc, cidst : pointer of byte);
var cippl, cipsmod, cipdmod, cipsize : integer; cipsrcp, cipdstp : pointer of byte;
begin
	cipsmod := 40*(NUM_PLANES-1);
	cipdmod := ROW_STRIDE - 40;
	cipsize := (256<<6) | 20;   // 20 words = 40 bytes = one plane's row width
	for cippl:=0 to NUM_PLANES do begin
		cipsrcp := cisrc + 40*cippl;
		cipdstp := cidst + 40*cippl;
		asm("
			lea $dff000,a6
			move.l Bobs_cipsrcp,a0
			move.l Bobs_cipdstp,a1
		CopyImageToScreenWait:
			btst #14,$dff002
			bne.s CopyImageToScreenWait
			move.l #$ffffffff,BLTAFWM(a6)
			move.w #$09f0,BLTCON0(a6)
			move.w #$0,BLTCON1(a6)
			move.w Bobs_cipsmod,BLTAMOD(a6)
			move.w Bobs_cipdmod,BLTDMOD(a6)
			move.l a0,BLTAPTH(a6)
			move.l a1,BLTDPTH(a6)
			move.w Bobs_cipsize,BLTSIZE(a6)
		");
	end;
end;

// One-time whole-screen capture into bg_pristine -- see bg_pristine's own
// declaration comment above. cscreen is passed in as a parameter (#screen
// in main.ras) rather than referenced by plain name, since #screen is
// declared in main.ras and this unit can't reach it otherwise (rule 1).
// "cscreen + 40*cppl" is a pointer-PARAMETER base plus ONE inline
// multiplication -- the same 2-term shape already proven safe by
// BlitStampMaskedPerPlane's "ppwmask + ppl*ppchunk" above. Quirk 3 in the
// compiler-quirks reference near the top of this file (the truncating-add
// bug) is specific to a pointer VARIABLE base in a 3-term expression --
// it does not apply to a parameter, nor to this simpler 2-term shape.
procedure CaptureBgPristine(cscreen : pointer of byte);
var cppl, cpmod, cpsize : integer; cpsrc, cpdst : pointer of byte;
begin
	cpmod := ROW_STRIDE - 40;
	cpsize := (256<<6) | 20;   // 20 words = 40 bytes = one plane's row width
	for cppl:=0 to DISPLAY_BITPLANES do begin
		cpsrc := cscreen + 40*cppl;
		cpdst := #bg_pristine + 40*cppl;
		asm("
			lea $dff000,a6
			move.l Bobs_cpsrc,a0
			move.l Bobs_cpdst,a1
		CaptureBgPristineWait:
			btst #14,$dff002
			bne.s CaptureBgPristineWait
			move.l #$ffffffff,BLTAFWM(a6)
			move.w #$09f0,BLTCON0(a6)
			move.w #$0,BLTCON1(a6)
			move.w Bobs_cpmod,BLTAMOD(a6)
			move.w Bobs_cpmod,BLTDMOD(a6)
			move.l a0,BLTAPTH(a6)
			move.l a1,BLTDPTH(a6)
			move.w Bobs_cpsize,BLTSIZE(a6)
		");
	end;
end;

// One-time startup copy of a caller's front buffer into its back buffer,
// so both buffers start out identical before any entity is ever drawn on
// either -- otherwise the back buffer's very first frame on screen (after
// the first flip) would show whatever uninitialized chip RAM it started
// with everywhere an entity hasn't yet drawn/restored over. Both source
// and destination are parameters for the same reason as CaptureBgPristine
// above (this unit can't reach a caller's plain-named buffer any other
// way -- rule 1 at the top of this file).
procedure CopyScreenToScreen2(cs2screen, cs2screen2 : pointer of byte);
var cs2pl, cs2mod, cs2size : integer; cs2src, cs2dst : pointer of byte;
begin
	cs2mod := ROW_STRIDE - 40;
	cs2size := (256<<6) | 20;
	for cs2pl:=0 to DISPLAY_BITPLANES do begin
		cs2src := cs2screen + 40*cs2pl;
		cs2dst := cs2screen2 + 40*cs2pl;
		asm("
			lea $dff000,a6
			move.l Bobs_cs2src,a0
			move.l Bobs_cs2dst,a1
		CopyScreenToScreen2Wait:
			btst #14,$dff002
			bne.s CopyScreenToScreen2Wait
			move.l #$ffffffff,BLTAFWM(a6)
			move.w #$09f0,BLTCON0(a6)
			move.w #$0,BLTCON1(a6)
			move.w Bobs_cs2mod,BLTAMOD(a6)
			move.w Bobs_cs2mod,BLTDMOD(a6)
			move.l a0,BLTAPTH(a6)
			move.l a1,BLTDPTH(a6)
			move.w Bobs_cs2size,BLTSIZE(a6)
		");
	end;
end;

// Pokes just the 3 bitplane-pointer copper slots (copper_bitplane0/1/2)
// directly, instead of calling Graphics::SetupDefaultScreen every frame to
// achieve the same pointer update. That built-in procedure also re-pokes
// copper_resolution and copper_mod_even/odd, and walks a bpl=5/4/3/2/1
// comparison chain, every single call -- none of which ever changes frame
// to frame once the display mode is set up; only the bitplane pointer
// itself differs between calls. This hand-rolls just the pointer pokes
// instead, using the exact two-word-pair write pattern
// (SetCopperList32-shaped) the compiler itself generates for a copper
// list entry -- confirmed by reading the compiled output. Hardcodes
// exactly 3 planes, matching NUM_PLANES=DISPLAY_BITPLANES=3 -- if
// DISPLAY_BITPLANES is ever raised for real, this needs a 4th/5th poke
// added, or the caller needs to fall back to calling
// Graphics::SetupDefaultScreen again instead of this; it will NOT
// silently do the right thing on its own. copper_bitplane0/1/2 are
// compiler-generated labels
// (part of the static copper list template), not user-declared Pascal
// vars -- referenced directly here with no "Bobs_" qualification.
// CONFIRMED (not just assumed) via direct main.asm inspection after this
// unit's first successful build: compiler-generated labels are NOT
// subject to the per-unit mangling rule 1 at the top of this file
// describes for user-declared identifiers -- "lea copper_bitplane0,a0"
// and "lea copper_custom,a5" both appear verbatim, unprefixed, inside
// this unit's own compiled code. Whether that's because they're emitted
// into a single shared namespace regardless of file, or some other
// mechanism, wasn't determined -- only that it works.
//
// WRITE-ORDER BUG, found and fixed under real timing pressure (a tutorial
// build with enough bobs to approach the NTSC frame budget): each
// copper_bitplaneN label is TWO separate copper MOVE instructions back to
// back with no WAIT between them -- confirmed via the compiled copper
// list itself:
//     copper_bitplane0:  dc.w $e0,0    (BPL1PTH -- register+data, offset 0/2)
//                        dc.w $e2,0    (BPL1PTL -- register+data, offset 4/6)
// The copper executes these in address order -- PTH's data word (offset 2)
// BEFORE PTL's data word (offset 6). The ORIGINAL version of this
// procedure wrote them in the OPPOSITE order (offset 6 first, then offset
// 2, with a "swap" instruction sitting between the two writes) -- a
// mismatch that is exactly the shape of a torn-pointer race: if the
// copper's own read of these two words happens to fall between the CPU's
// two writes, it loads the OLD PTH combined with the NEW PTL (or vice
// versa on some other interleaving), producing a corrupted, half-updated
// bitplane address. Agnus then fetches that plane's data from that wrong
// address for the rest of the frame -- which showed up on screen as
// recognizable sprite/mask content (still real chip RAM, often still part
// of the intended buffer) landing at the wrong row, spread across the
// display width as multiple bobs each contributed a fragment.
//
// This only manifests when FlipBuffer's poke happens LATE in the frame --
// i.e. when the whole per-frame pipeline is close to (or over) the frame
// budget, leaving little margin before the copper next reaches these
// instructions. Confirmed via direct A/B testing: clean under PAL (more
// headroom) and at lower enemy counts, corrupted under NTSC with enough
// bobs active to push the frame close to full. This is the general class
// of hazard any live copper-list poke runs: an unthrottled write racing
// the copper's own read of the same data, made worse here because nothing
// constrains WHEN within the frame an otherwise-correct interrupt-driven
// pipeline reaches its own flip -- the closer to the frame's end it runs,
// the less margin is left before the copper's own next read of these
// exact words.
//
// FIX: write PTH's data word (offset 2) BEFORE PTL's data word (offset 6)
// -- matching the copper's own read order -- with NO instruction between
// the two writes (both halves of the address are computed into separate
// registers, d0/d1, before either write happens). This doesn't
// mathematically prove the race is impossible under every conceivable
// timing, but it eliminates the specific inverted-order mismatch that
// made it easy to trigger, and matches the standard technique for this
// kind of live copper-list poke.
procedure FlipToBuffer(fbbuf : pointer of byte);
begin
	asm("
		move.l Bobs_fbbuf,d0
		move.l d0,d1
		swap d1
		lea copper_bitplane0,a0
		move.w d1,2(a0)
		move.w d0,6(a0)
		move.l Bobs_fbbuf,d0
		add.l #40,d0
		move.l d0,d1
		swap d1
		lea copper_bitplane1,a0
		move.w d1,2(a0)
		move.w d0,6(a0)
		move.l Bobs_fbbuf,d0
		add.l #80,d0
		move.l d0,d1
		swap d1
		lea copper_bitplane2,a0
		move.w d1,2(a0)
		move.w d0,6(a0)
	");
end;

// Self-contained double-buffering flip: makes cur_buf's buffer (the one
// this frame just finished drawing into) the new FRONT/displayed buffer,
// then flips cur_buf/draw_target so the NEXT frame draws into the buffer
// that is now safely off-screen again. Callers with no other per-buffer
// state to manage (see this section's own header comment) can use this
// directly -- Bobs::FlipBuffer(#screen, #screen2) -- with no bookkeeping
// of their own beyond that single call.
//
// The BBUSY wait first is deliberate, not decorative: a masked/shifted
// draw call triggers its last blit and returns immediately, so the last
// blit into this buffer may still be running -- waiting here guarantees
// the buffer is fully, consistently drawn before it can become visible.
//
// The flip itself pokes the copper's own bitplane-pointer words directly
// (FlipToBuffer above) -- a handful of register pokes, not a data-moving
// operation, so doing this every frame costs nothing like a screen-sized
// blit would. The copper re-executes those pointer words once per real
// vertical blank, and this poke's effect is normally only visible
// starting the NEXT vblank -- by which point the blit this procedure just
// waited for has long since finished, so there's nothing left for the
// beam to tear.
//
// CALL THIS LAST IN YOUR INTERRUPT HANDLER, after Restore/Update/Draw --
// NOT first, despite that being the obvious-looking fix for the write-
// order race documented in FlipToBuffer's own comment (whose margin
// shrinks as a frame's workload grows). That was tried directly and
// reverted after live testing in emulation: calling FlipBuffer as the
// very FIRST thing in the handler made the "only visible starting the
// NEXT vblank" assumption above stop holding -- the newly-flipped-to back
// buffer became visible in the SAME frame draws started landing on it,
// i.e. the poke won the race against the copper's read for the frame
// that was already starting, not the following one. Exact hardware
// mechanism not root-caused (this project's working theory: VERTB and
// the copper's own list restart are close enough in timing that a poke
// issued with minimal interrupt-entry latency can beat the copper to
// these words, where one issued after a frame's worth of blitter-bound
// work reliably can't) -- but the empirical, tested conclusion is firm:
// keep this call LAST. If the write-order race's margin is too tight at
// your workload, look to speeding up what runs before this call (see
// BlitterNastyOn's own comment for one attempt) rather than moving this
// call earlier.
procedure FlipBuffer(fbscreen, fbscreen2 : pointer of byte);
begin
	asm("
		FlipBufferSelfWait:
		btst #14,$dff002
		bne.s FlipBufferSelfWait
	");
	if (cur_buf=0) then begin
		FlipToBuffer(fbscreen);
		cur_buf := 1;
		draw_target := fbscreen2;
	end else begin
		FlipToBuffer(fbscreen2);
		cur_buf := 0;
		draw_target := fbscreen;
	end;
end;

// ============================================================================
// "Blitter Nasty" -- BLTPRI, DMACON bit 10 ($0400): while a blit is
// running, setting this bit makes the blitter win every bus cycle it
// contests against the CPU, instead of sharing turn-and-turn-about. It
// changes NOTHING about the copper, bitplane fetch, sprites, audio, or
// disk DMA -- all of those already outrank the blitter in the Amiga's
// fixed DMA priority chain regardless of this bit, so the display and the
// copper program can never be disrupted by it. The CPU is the only
// affected party, and only for the duration of an actual blit -- BLTPRI is
// completely inert whenever the blitter is idle.
//
// COMMON MISTAKE, worth flagging explicitly: BLTCON1 bit 1 is NOT a
// priority bit -- that bit is DESC (descending-mode addressing, which
// runs the blitter backwards through its pointers). Setting DESC while
// the pointers are still configured for a normal ascending blit produces
// garbage output that looks nothing like a priority/timing problem.
// BLTPRI lives only in DMACON, at bit 10 ($0400) -- a completely different
// register. DMACON uses the standard Amiga SET/CLR convention: writing a
// value with bit 15 set OR's the given bits in (SET), writing one with
// bit 15 clear AND-clears them (CLR) -- so "move.w #$8400,$dff096" sets
// BLTPRI and "move.w #$0400,$dff096" clears it; both are bit-masked writes
// that cannot disturb DMAEN/BPLEN/COPEN/BLTEN or any other DMACON bit.
//
// WHY THIS IS SAFE TO USE HEAVILY: every blit issued by this unit is
// finite and short, and the CPU has nothing else productive to do while
// waiting for one -- it's spinning in a BBUSY busy-wait (a tight
// "btst #14,$dff002 / bne.s" loop) for the blit to finish anyway. Handing
// the blitter absolute priority during that wait costs the CPU nothing it
// was going to use productively, and shortens how long the blit itself
// takes to complete under contention. There is no starvation or deadlock
// scenario to construct here, since nothing ever waits on the CPU to make
// progress while BLTPRI is set -- worst case the CPU runs slightly slower
// for the duration of one blit.
//
// TWO WAYS TO SCOPE IT: BlitterNastyOn/Off below bracket it around a block
// of code (CPU-scoped) -- simple, and correct as long as the CPU has
// genuinely idle time during that block. InstallNastyCopper further down
// instead has the COPPER clear BLTPRI at a fixed early raster line and set
// it again at a later one (nasty_on_line) -- raster-scoped, enforced by
// hardware rather than by code reaching a particular line, so a blit that
// overruns into the visible display window can't leave BLTPRI stuck on by
// accident. Raster-scoping is the one to prefer once other CPU work (input
// polling, game logic) starts overlapping the blits, since it guarantees
// the CPU gets its normal share back for the visible portion of the frame
// regardless of how the surrounding code is structured.
//
// MEASURED RESULT (this project's own finding, worth passing on): enabling
// BLTPRI barely changed raw blit throughput -- if the CPU isn't executing
// from a bus-contending memory region, its BBUSY-poll loop may not be
// stealing enough cycles from the blitter for this to matter much either
// way. What it DID fix was tearing/non-determinism: without it, a blit
// could be caught mid-operation by the raster beam or by another
// contending access, producing a partially-written sprite for one frame.
// With BLTPRI on, each blit completes as one uninterrupted, deterministic
// burst. So treat this primarily as a correctness/determinism tool for
// blits that share the bus with other contending activity, not as a
// guaranteed throughput win -- measure on your own target before relying
// on a speed improvement.
//
// SECOND MEASUREMENT, a different project and a different question
// (tutorial.ras, testing whether BLTPRI could widen the margin on the
// copper write-order race documented in FlipToBuffer's own comment, by
// speeding up the blitter-bound work that runs before FlipBuffer's own
// poke): enabling it by default made things WORSE, not better, at the
// workload's edge (9 bobs, right where that race's margin was already
// thin) -- visibly MORE unstable artifacts with BLTPRI on than off,
// tested live in emulation. At a comfortable bob count (8, below the
// edge) it measured no different either way, consistent with the first
// finding above. Root mechanism not established -- worth flagging one
// plausible direction rather than leaving it a pure mystery: BLTPRI only
// changes who wins CONTESTED cycles, it doesn't create bus bandwidth, so
// if the CPU's own BBUSY-poll loop was providing some incidental, non-
// obvious timing regularity that happened to keep FlipBuffer's poke
// falling in a safe part of the frame, removing that regularity (by
// letting the blitter run less interrupted, shifting exactly when each
// blit's BBUSY wait resolves) could plausibly move the poke's timing
// AROUND rather than simply earlier -- occasionally into the danger
// window rather than reliably out of it. Speculative, not confirmed.
// Net conclusion for this project: BLTPRI is not a reliable fix for the
// write-order race specifically, and given it measured somewhere between
// no-help and actively-worse for that exact use, tutorial.ras leaves it
// out entirely rather than enabling it as a mitigation.
// ============================================================================

// Give the blitter absolute bus priority. Written as a raw immediate
// rather than through a TRSE variable deliberately -- $8400 has its high
// bit set (reads as negative in 16-bit signed terms), exactly the shape
// Quirk 5 in the compiler-quirks reference near the top of this file
// miscompiles when it goes through constant-assignment codegen inside a
// branch.
//
// TO ENABLE THIS BY DEFAULT FOR A WHOLE PROGRAM RUN: call
// Bobs::BlitterNastyOn() ONCE from the caller's own Main() setup, before
// enabling interrupts, and simply never call BlitterNastyOff -- there is
// no per-frame maintenance needed (DMACON just holds whatever it was last
// set to). Can't be wired to happen automatically from inside FlipBuffer
// itself despite that being the more "zero caller changes needed" option
// -- FlipBuffer is declared earlier in this file than this procedure, and
// Quirk 7 in the compiler-quirks reference confirms a same-unit call to a
// later-declared procedure fails to compile at all.
procedure BlitterNastyOn();
begin
	asm("
		move.w #$8400,$dff096
	");
end;

// Hand the CPU its normal share of the bus back. The BBUSY spin first is
// deliberate: a caller's own draw call typically triggers its last blit
// and returns immediately without waiting for it to finish, so clearing
// BLTPRI right away -- before that blit has actually completed -- would
// drop blitter priority for the tail end of an operation that's still
// running, right when contention is most likely to slow it down.
procedure BlitterNastyOff();
begin
	asm("
		NastyOffWait:
		btst #14,$dff002
		bne.s NastyOffWait
		move.w #$0400,$dff096
	");
end;

// Mode 1: hand the whole BLTPRI window over to the copper, so it toggles
// automatically at fixed raster positions every frame with ZERO per-frame
// CPU maintenance -- unlike, say, a bitplane pointer register that
// auto-increments and needs re-poking every frame, DMACON just sits at
// whatever it was last set to, so writing this once at startup is enough
// forever.
//
// copper_custom is a compiler-generated label -- the static copper list's
// spare/reusable template block -- referenced directly with no "Bobs_"
// qualification, same reasoning as FlipToBuffer's copper_bitplaneN
// references above (compiler-generated labels live in one shared
// namespace, not subject to this unit's own identifier-mangling rules).
// It is positioned in the compiler's static copper list immediately after
// a "WAIT for raster line 44" / "set BPLCON0" instruction pair that's
// already part of every screen setup this project uses -- so by the time
// the copper reaches copper_custom, it has just reached line 44 of the
// CURRENT frame.
//
// A copper program is just a flat sequence of two-word instructions, each
// either a WAIT or a MOVE, distinguished by bit 0 of the first word (1 =
// WAIT, 0 = MOVE). This procedure writes 5 such instructions (10 words =
// 20 bytes, deliberately fitting inside the 16-byte spare block this
// project's copper_custom template reserves without overflowing into
// whatever follows it in the compiled list):
//   1. MOVE $0400 into register offset $096 (DMACON) -- CLEARS BLTPRI
//      (bit 15 of the data word is 0, so this is the "AND-clear" form of
//      DMACON's SET/CLR convention). Runs immediately, at line 44 --
//      handing the CPU normal bus priority for the rest of the visible
//      display.
//   2. A WAIT instruction for raster line nasty_on_line: encoded as
//      (nasty_on_line<<8)|1 for its first word (VP in the high byte, with
//      bit 0 set to mark it as a WAIT rather than a MOVE) and $fffe for
//      its second (the compare mask -- ignore horizontal position,
//      compare vertical position only). The copper simply idles at this
//      instruction until the raster beam reaches that line.
//   3. MOVE $8400 into register $096 (DMACON) again -- SETS BLTPRI this
//      time (bit 15 of the data word is 1, the "OR-set" form) -- handing
//      the blitter absolute priority from nasty_on_line through to the
//      bottom of the frame and the following vertical blank, which is
//      where all of this project's per-frame blitting happens.
//   4. A final terminator word pair ($fffffffe as a longword, i.e.
//      $ffff,$fffe) -- a WAIT for a raster/horizontal position that a
//      normal frame's beam never reaches, standard practice for safely
//      ending a short inline copper block so it just idles rather than
//      falling through into whatever instruction happens to follow it in
//      memory.
// Register $096 is comfortably above the copper's own "danger threshold"
// (by default the copper refuses to write registers below $080 unless
// explicitly permitted), so no CDANG/COPCON change is needed for any of
// this to work.
procedure InstallNastyCopper();
begin
	asm("
		lea copper_custom,a5
		move.w #$0096,(a5)+
		move.w #$0400,(a5)+
		move.w Bobs_nasty_on_line,d0
		lsl.w #8,d0
		or.w #$0001,d0
		move.w d0,(a5)+
		move.w #$fffe,(a5)+
		move.w #$0096,(a5)+
		move.w #$8400,(a5)+
		move.l #$fffffffe,(a5)+
	");
end;

// ============================================================================
// GENERAL-PURPOSE HELPERS -- flagged as POTENTIALLY BELONGING IN ANOTHER
// LIBRARY, not this one.
//
// IdleUntilInterrupt and ProfMark below have nothing to do with drawing or
// erasing blitter objects -- they're generic 68000/Amiga utilities (a CPU
// idle-halt, and a one-instruction raster-timing profiler) that simply
// happened to live in the same source file as the bob mechanism before
// this unit was extracted from it, and were carried over here along with
// everything else rather than left behind or given a third home of their
// own. Noting this explicitly for whoever ends up organizing this as a
// shared, multi-unit TRSE library: these two arguably belong in a more
// general-purpose unit (something like a "system.tru" or "debug.tru", if
// one exists or gets created) rather than in a unit whose name and header
// comment are specifically about blitter objects. They're kept here for
// now, in their own clearly separate section, since this project currently
// only has the one shared unit -- move them freely whenever a better home
// exists; nothing else in this unit depends on them.
// ============================================================================

// Genuine CPU-halt for an otherwise-empty main loop: the 68000 STOP
// instruction loads the given 16-bit value into SR, then stops
// fetching/executing entirely until ANY interrupt whose priority exceeds
// the resulting IPL mask occurs, at which point it resumes at the
// instruction right after STOP -- unlike an empty while-loop, the CPU is
// not executing NOP-equivalent iterations while idle, it is doing nothing
// at all, which frees real bus bandwidth/power for the rest of the
// system while there's genuinely nothing for the CPU to do.
//
// $2000 = supervisor bit (13) set, IPL mask (bits 8-10) at 0, trace (15)
// off, condition codes cleared. IPL=0 ("wake on anything") sidesteps
// needing to know any particular interrupt source's exact hardware
// priority level -- if interrupts are already firing and being serviced
// correctly elsewhere in the calling program, the CPU's own interrupt
// mask is necessarily already low enough for that to happen, and 0 is
// the lowest it can be, so this can only be at least as permissive as
// whatever's already working.
//
// STOP is PRIVILEGED -- requires supervisor mode. If a program using this
// crashes or guru-meditates the instant it's reached, that's the first
// thing to suspect: the program may be running in user mode, in which
// case STOP needs a supervisor-mode transition first, rather than being
// usable directly here.
procedure IdleUntilInterrupt();
begin
	asm("
		stop #$2000
	");
end;

// Writes a 12-bit Amiga color ($0RGB) directly to COLOR00 ($dff180), the
// border/background hardware color register -- one instruction, no loops,
// no arrays, no screen-memory pointer arithmetic, so there's nothing for a
// pointer-arithmetic bug to grab onto even under heavy debugging use. The
// color takes effect the instant the CPU executes it and holds until the
// next write (the caller's own next call, or the copper's own per-frame
// palette application at the top of the next frame) -- so calling this
// with a distinct color at each phase boundary in a main loop or interrupt
// handler turns the border into a live bar chart: each phase's color
// occupies the border for exactly as many raster lines as that phase took
// to run, visible directly as colored band height. No copper list changes
// needed -- the copper isn't involved in the timing at all, it's just
// continuously displaying whatever COLOR00 currently holds as the beam
// scans past.
procedure ProfMark(pmcolor : integer);
begin
	asm("
		move.w Bobs_pmcolor,$dff180
	");
end;

end.
